{-
   What a **lending** object's value refuses - `doc/inflight/Design-Iterator-Objects.md` §3.3, and
   §2.6's "a lent value lives until the next step".

   Nothing here is a rule of its own. A producer whose hand-over lends keeps the value in the record
   and the slot holds its address, so what a consumer is given is a borrow **rooted in the object** -
   `f$item`'s signature says so. Every refusal below is the loan checker's ordinary one: the next
   step needs the record whole.

   `test/resolve/fiber/Reject.Lend.yana` said the same thing about the stack handle, through
   `LentIter.current`'s signature; it is gone with the handle (2026-09-16) and this file is what it
   was superseded by rather than what it was rewritten into - every claim it made is one of these,
   and the class half below is one it could not make at all.

   **And through the class it is the same refusal**, which is what the borrowing `Iterate` is for:
   `next(&self: source's) -> Maybe(source'item)` roots the answer in the object, so a body that holds
   what one step gave it across the next is refused by the rule that refuses `for h in it: for g in
   it`. That is §3.3 stated once and enforced in both spellings, rather than a rule of the feature's
   own - which is why this file holds a concrete `for` and a class-generic body side by side.
-}

data Held {id: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->value: Held): {}

iter fn lent(n: Int) -> Held:
    let &i = 1 :: Int

    while i <= n:
        let h = Held {id: i}
        yield h
        i = i + 1

-- Stepping the object again from inside the loop whose variable borrows it.
fn stepInsideLoop() -> Int:
    let &kept = 0 :: Int
    let &it = lent(3)

    for h in it:
        for g in it:
            kept = kept + g.id + h.id

    return kept

-- And borrowing the object exclusively while a value it lent is still live, which is what any
-- second step is.
fn writeWhileLent() -> Int:
    let &kept = 0 :: Int
    let &it = lent(3)

    for h in it:
        let &other = it
        kept = kept + h.id

    return kept

{-
   The same thing a body compiled once says, where `it` is a type variable and the only thing known
   about it is that it steps.

   `held` is a loan of the object, so the second `next()` is a second exclusive borrow of something
   already borrowed. No message of this feature's is involved: the constraint gives the body the
   class's signature and the signature is what publishes the origin.
-}
fn (Iterate(s, Held, {})) twoAtOnce(&it: s) -> Int:
    let &kept = 0 :: Int

    if it.next() is Just(held):
        if it.next() is Just(second):
            kept = held.id + second.id

    return kept

fn throughClass() -> Int:
    let &it = lent(3)
    return twoAtOnce(it)

-- And keeping one past the step that replaces it, which is the same fact said about a binding.
fn (Iterate(s, Held, {})) keptPastStep(&it: s) -> Int:
    let &kept = 0 :: Int
    let seen = it.next()

    if it.next() is Just(second):
        kept = second.id

    if seen is Just(first):
        kept = kept + first.id

    return kept

fn holding() -> Int:
    let &it = lent(3)
    return keptPastStep(it)

fn main() -> Int = stepInsideLoop() + writeWhileLent() + throughClass() + holding()
